Multiple instructions are in flight simultaneously.

Problem Introduced by Pipelines
Pipelines improve performance but complicate execution.

Suppose:

a = 1;
b = 2;

The CPU may overlap parts of these instructions internally.

More importantly, when instructions are independent, processors and compilers may execute them in an order different from the source code while preserving the behavior of a single-threaded program.

This optimization is called instruction reordering.

We'll study it in depth in Lesson 5, because it's one of the primary reasons the Java Memory Model exists.

What We've Learned So Far
We now understand:

A CPU is composed of cores, registers, ALUs, control logic, and caches.
Registers are the fastest storage and hold values currently being processed.
The ALU performs arithmetic and logical operations.
The Control Unit coordinates instruction execution.
Every instruction goes through the Fetch–Decode–Execute cycle.
Modern CPUs use pipelines to execute many instruction stages concurrently.
Multiple cores execute different Java threads in parallel.
Because each core has its own local state (registers and private caches), sharing data between threads is not automatic.
The Question That Leads to Lesson 3
Suppose two CPU cores execute:

balance++;

Where does balance actually live?

In a register?
In L1 cache?
In L2 cache?
In L3 cache?
In RAM?
And if one core changes it, how does the other core eventually see the new value?

Answering those questions requires understanding the memory hierarchy and CPU caches.


Lesson 3 — Memory Hierarchy: Why CPU Caches Exist
Goal: By the end of this lesson, you'll understand why CPUs need caches, how data moves through the memory hierarchy, why stale data occurs, and why this is the root cause of the Java Memory Model (JMM).

This is arguably the most important prerequisite for JMM. If you deeply understand this lesson, volatile, synchronized, CAS, and memory barriers will make much more sense.

Chapter 1 — The CPU-RAM Speed Mismatch
Let's start with a fundamental question.

If RAM stores all your data, why doesn't the CPU read directly from RAM every time?

Because RAM is far too slow compared to the CPU.

Imagine:

You can solve 1 math problem every second.
But every time you need a number, you have to wait 2 minutes for someone to bring it to you.
Would your speed matter?

No.

You'd spend almost all your time waiting.

This is exactly what happens in computers.

Modern CPU Speed
A 4 GHz CPU runs approximately:

4,000,000,000 cycles per second

One clock cycle:

≈ 0.25 nanoseconds

So every quarter of a nanosecond, the CPU is ready for another instruction.

RAM Speed
Accessing DRAM typically takes around:

60–100+ nanoseconds

That doesn't sound like much.

But compare them.

Component	Approximate Latency
Register	~1 cycle
L1 Cache	~4 cycles
L2 Cache	~10–15 cycles
L3 Cache	~30–60 cycles
DRAM	~200–400 cycles (architecture-dependent)

A CPU can execute many instructions while waiting for one RAM access.

Analogy
Imagine:

CPU = Ferrari

RAM = Bicycle

Ferrari

↓

Stops

↓

Waits

↓

Bicycle arrives

↓

Ferrari moves again

The Ferrari isn't slow.

It's constantly waiting.

Engineers call this the memory wall—the growing performance gap between CPU speed and main memory latency.

