The Java Memory Model defines when changes made by one thread must become visible to another, and constructs like volatile and synchronized are how Java programmers express those requirements.

Summary
Today we've established several key ideas:

CPU cores are much faster than RAM, so they rely on caches.
Caches hold copies of data, not the authoritative version.
Data moves between memory and caches in cache lines (commonly 64 bytes).
Cache hits are fast; cache misses are expensive.
Each CPU core has its own private caches, so different cores can temporarily hold different copies of the same shared data.
This temporary divergence is the fundamental reason visibility problems exist in multithreaded programs.


