Why Context Switching Is Expensive
Many developers think:

"Switching threads is free."

Not even close.

Every context switch involves work.

The operating system must:

Enter kernel mode
Save CPU registers
Save the Program Counter
Save stack pointers
Potentially update memory-management state
Choose another runnable thread
Restore the new thread's registers
Restore the Program Counter
Return to user mode
During this period:

No useful application work is being done.

The CPU is busy managing execution instead of progressing your business logic.

