Context Switching vs Process Switching
Earlier we learned that threads share process resources.

This now explains an important fact.

Switching between threads of the same process usually requires less work than switching between different processes.

Why?

Because threads already share:

Heap
Code
Open files
Native libraries
The operating system mainly changes the execution context.

A process switch may also involve changing the virtual address space and other process-level state, making it generally more expensive.

This is one reason multithreading became popular.

A Real-World Analogy
Imagine one chef in a restaurant.

Orders:

Pizza

Burger

Pasta

