The same is true for CPUs.

Key Takeaways
A single CPU core executes one instruction stream at a time.
Concurrency is the illusion of simultaneous progress created by rapid scheduling.
Parallelism is true simultaneous execution on multiple CPU cores.
The operating system scheduler decides which runnable thread executes next.
A context switch saves one thread's execution context and restores another's.
Context switches have real costs: scheduler work, kernel transitions, and reduced cache locality.
Creating more threads than the system can efficiently schedule often decreases performance rather than increasing it.


