The "As If Serial" Principle
A very important compiler concept.

The JVM can reorder operations as long as:

Single-thread behavior remains unchanged.

Example:

int a = 10;

int b = 20;

Compiler may reorder:

b = 20;

a = 10;

because:

One thread sees same result.

But with multiple threads:

Observable behavior may change.

JMM restricts unsafe reorderings.

Data Race
A data race occurs when:

Two threads access the same variable.
At least one access is a write.
No ordering guarantee exists.
Example:

int count;


Thread A:

count++;


Thread B:

count++;

This has a data race.

