There is no happens-before relationship.

Therefore:

Thread 2 is allowed to see:

0

even after Thread 1 wrote:

42

Happens-Before Rules
The JMM defines several rules.

These are the foundation:

Program Order Rule
Monitor Lock Rule
Volatile Variable Rule
Thread Start Rule
Thread Join Rule
Interrupt Rule
Final Field Rule
Transitivity Rule
Let's study each deeply.

Rule 1 — Program Order Rule
Definition
Within a single thread:

Every action in a thread happens-before every action that comes later in that same thread.

Example:

int a = 10;

int b = 20;

The JVM guarantees:

a = 10

happens-before

b = 20

Important Point
This only applies inside one thread.

Example:

Thread 1:

x = 10;

Thread 2:

print(x);

No relationship exists.

Example
Single thread:

void method(){

    int x = 10;

    int y = x + 20;

}

Execution:

x=10

↓

y=30

The compiler may optimize internally.

But the result must appear equivalent.

Rule 2 — Monitor Lock Rule
This is the foundation of:

synchronized

Definition
An unlock on a monitor happens-before every subsequent lock on the same monitor.

Meaning:

Thread A

lock

write data

unlock


        happens-before


Thread B

lock

read data

Example
class Counter {

    private int count;


    synchronized void increment(){

        count++;

    }


    synchronized int get(){

        return count;

    }

}

Thread A:

increment();

Thread B:

get();

Execution:

Thread A:

lock

count++

unlock

Thread B:

lock

read count

unlock

Because:

unlock
   |
   |
   v
lock

Thread B sees the updated count.

Why Unlock Creates Visibility
When a thread exits:

synchronized(lock){

}

the JVM performs:

Release barrier

↓

Make writes visible

Another thread entering:

synchronized(lock)

performs:

Acquire barrier

↓

Read latest values

Rule 3 — Volatile Variable Rule
This is one of the most important rules.

Definition
A write to a volatile variable happens-before every subsequent read of that variable.

Example:

volatile boolean ready;

Thread A:

data = 100;

ready = true;

Thread B:

if(ready){

    System.out.println(data);

}

Without volatile
Possible:

Thread A:

data=100

ready=true


Thread B:

ready=true

data=0

With volatile
The relationship becomes:

Thread A:

data=100

ready=true


        happens-before


Thread B:

read ready

read data

Guarantee:

data == 100

